# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
boethius-foreknowledge.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r3 and boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r3.2 have nothing from it; boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r3.3 has "□occurs → ¬free"; they differ when only "there is freedom of the will" and "necessarily, that which providence foresees to be about to happen comes to pass" are true, which the first allows
boethius-foreknowledge.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S3 is read boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r3 and boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r3.2 with no modality; boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r3.3 as what is necessary or possible: its "can" does not say which it means
boethius-foreknowledge.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "there is freedom of the will" and "necessarily, that which providence foresees to be about to happen comes to pass" are true. Is S3, "Wherefore, if from eternity He foreknows not only what men will do, but also their designs and purposes, there can be no freedom of the will, seeing that nothing can be done, nor can any sort of purpose be entertained, save such as a Divine providence, incapable of being deceived, has perceived beforehand.", true then?
boethius-foreknowledge.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r3 has "◇¬occurs → ¬sure" and "impious" and "impious → sure"; boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r3.2 and boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r3.3 have "◇¬occurs → ¬sure" and "impious"; they differ when only "to think of God that He has only uncertain conjecture of the future is impiety" is true, which the second allows
boethius-foreknowledge.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered yes, which keeps boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r3.2 and boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "to think of God that He has only uncertain conjecture of the future is impiety" is true and "there is sure foreknowledge of the future", "possibly, it is not so that that which providence foresees to be about to happen comes to pass", "possibly, it is not so that that which providence foresees to be about to happen comes to pass" and "possibly, it is not so that that which providence foresees to be about to happen comes to pass" are false. Is S4, "For if the issues can be turned aside to some other end than that foreseen by providence, there will not then be any sure foreknowledge of the future, but uncertain conjecture instead, and to think this of God I deem impiety.", true then?
boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r3: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r3 and boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r3.2 have "free → ◇¬occurs"; boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r3.3 has "foreknows → sure"; they differ when only "God foresees everything and can in no wise be deceived, foreknowing from eternity what men will do and also their designs and purposes", "there is freedom of the will" and "possibly, it is not so that that which providence foresees to be about to happen comes to pass" are true, which the first allows
boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r3: argue/readings: U1: boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r3 and boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r3.3 take for granted "if God foresees everything and can in no wise be deceived, foreknowing from eternity what men will do and also their designs and purposes, then there is sure foreknowledge of the future"; boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r3.2 does not
boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r3: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r3 and boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r3.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if God foresees everything and can in no wise be deceived, foreknowing from eternity what men will do and also their designs and purposes, then there is sure foreknowledge of the future"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
boethius-foreknowledge.md:1: argue/converse: boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r3 C (S3): the sentence makes the words after "no" the condition, but C has them follow from it: it reads "if God foresees everything and can in no wise be deceived, foreknowing from eternity what men will do and also their designs and purposes, then it is not so that there is freedom of the will"
